{
  "story_id": "S077",
  "title": "TUBA1A depletion induces compensatory beta-tubulin and Tuba4a transcripts in thalamic and Pvalb neurons",
  "status": "grounded",
  "rejection_reason": null,
  "exact_observed_result": "In 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut, Tuba1a was depleted with logFC -0.967 and FDR 2.78e-20 while Tubb3, Tubb2a, Tuba4a, Tubb4a, Tubb4b, and Tubb5 rose at FDR<0.05. Pvalb GABA neurons reproduced the same direction with Tuba1a logFC -0.674 FDR 0.0151 and Tubb2a/Tuba4a/Tubb3 induction. Tbcd and Tbce perturbations separately induced Tuba1a across several caudal classes.",
  "biological_interpretation": "Neurons activate transcript-level tubulin compensation after losing the dominant alpha-tubulin Tuba1a, whereas individual beta-tubulin or Tuba4a perturbations do not simply reciprocate; tubulin-folding cofactor perturbations expose a related but opposite Tuba1a induction axis.",
